WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.160
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.480
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.760
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.000
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.820
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.180
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.800
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:20.980
We're The Office ladies.

9
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:24.440
Hello, everybody.

10
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.480
There is a new paper company in town.

11
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.860
Michael Scott Paper Company.

12
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.060
Holy moly.

13
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.780
We're going to talk about it today.

14
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.400
However, I have to say this before we get started.

15
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:36.980
What?

16
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.020
We look gorgeous today.

17
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:41.040
You all should know.

18
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.900
I don't know what this podcast is going to be today, but we look hot.

19
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.520
Well, Jenna has that like dark, smoky eyeliner.

20
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.140
I did a smoky eye today.

21
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.560
How do you even do that?

22
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:53.760
I need a tutorial.

23
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.620
Oh, multiple YouTube tutorials is how we got this smoky eye today.

24
00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.320
I did super straight hair.

25
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.540
You look amazing.

26
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.580
You look like you're about to take over like multiple corporations.

27
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:06.960
I am wearing a blazer.

28
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:07.980
I know.

29
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.920
I guess we have to say that because people can't see us, but we look amazing.

30
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.720
Angela, your nails look incredible.

31
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:15.840
I mean, details.

32
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.900
We have details.

33
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:17.900
We look good, guys.

34
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.200
We're feeling it today.

35
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.260
We cannot wait to talk about this episode.

36
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.980
And then we have a photo shoot.

37
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.100
Jenna, who are we?

38
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:24.820
What is happening?

39
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.140
We're a big deal.

40
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.780
I don't know.

41
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.620
Will you tell my kids that next time we're at Target?

42
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.840
Hey, hey.

43
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.820
Big deal over there in the seasonal aisle.

44
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.480
Yeah, and you can only pick one of those things.

45
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.320
Pick one.

46
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.520
You're not getting all three.

47
00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.040
Let's get to talking about what we're here for.

48
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:51.100
It's season five, episode 23, written by the amazing Justin Spitzer and directed by Gene

49
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:51.920
Stupnitsky.

50
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.140
He's the credited director on this one, but he actually had a partner.

51
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:03.120
This episode was co-directed by Gene Stupnitsky and his longtime writing partner, Lee Eisenberg.

52
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.000
That's right.

53
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.300
They would go on to direct many more episodes together of The Office.

54
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.360
They shared a little bit in the commentary that they would rotate who would take credit.

55
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.340
It sort of went along some DGA rules at the time.

56
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.500
So Gene got credit for this one, but they were both there together every day.

57
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.240
So we want to give Lee a shout out.

58
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.160
And then the next time there's an episode up that says Lee directed, know that Gene was

59
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.140
right there by his side.

60
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.160
And we reached out to both of them, and they shared a ton of great details for this episode.

61
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.120
So I'm very, very excited.

62
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.920
Here's your summary.

63
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.080
Michael, Pam, and Ryan are in their new office.

64
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.200
They're struggling to adjuSt.

65
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.880
It's a very tiny, intimate work environment.

66
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.260
Very tiny.

67
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.840
Can I tell you one thing?

68
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.400
Justin said they even considered finding a smaller room.

69
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:51.880
What?

70
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.640
Can you imagine?

71
00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:59.000
I can't because I was there, and I know how almost impossible it was to shoot in that room.

72
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.520
With like the camera operators, the sound department, you guys.

73
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.020
Smaller room.

74
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.020
That's bonkers to me.

75
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.980
Well, upstairs in Dunder Mifflin, there is a new receptionist in town.

76
00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.340
A new lady named Kelly Erin Hannon.

77
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:22.040
Dwight and Andy are both kind of crushing on her, but they also have this new friendship.

78
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.600
So this crush is going to really challenge their new bond.

79
00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.480
In the meantime, Jim is trying to figure out what the heck is a rundown?

80
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.000
What is a rundown?

81
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.120
Poor guy.

82
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.260
And let's not forget, there's going to be some giant square pancakes.

83
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:39.240
Yeah.

84
00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.900
Lady, I forgot that they were big squares.

85
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.900
I have details on that.

86
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:44.480
You do?

87
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:44.780
I do.

88
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:45.620
Oh, I can't wait.

89
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.120
All right.

90
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.380
I have some pancake details.

91
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.060
I can't wait.

92
00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.360
Let's get into some fast facts.

93
00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:49.960
All right.

94
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.340
Here's a mini fast fact.

95
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.180
This episode actually aired the same night as Dream Team.

96
00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:01.080
In between these two episodes, they ran the very first episode of Parks and Recreation.

97
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:02.300
How about that?

98
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.340
We were like a little sandwich.

99
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.860
We were, and we were the bread, and Parks and Recreation was the meat.

100
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:08.640
Bologna.

101
00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:09.940
The cheese?

102
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:10.440
Cheese.

103
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:11.520
Sounds like a dig.

104
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:12.400
We don't mean that.

105
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.460
No, I didn't mean it to be.

106
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.100
I had a bologna sandwich every day in my lunch growing up.

107
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.720
No, but it's, you know, saying something is bologna or something is cheese.

108
00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.400
It seems like a dig, but...

109
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.160
They were the meat.

110
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.560
Well, now that seems like a dig on the bread.

111
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:27.780
Well, you can't.

112
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:28.620
As if the bread isn't important.

113
00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.060
Well, the bread's the best part.

114
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.680
That's just because I love bread.

115
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.600
Okay, we'll leave this analogy.

116
00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:36.740
There you have it.

117
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.800
It was office, Parks and Rec office.

118
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:39.300
Yes.

119
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.820
Fast fact number one, I thought we should talk a little bit about this teeny tiny space

120
00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.180
of Michael Scott Paper Company.

121
00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:53.820
This 165 square foot office with no windows on the bottom floor of Dunder Mifflin.

122
00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:56.920
With a exposed shower in the corner?

123
00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.140
Yes, correct.

124
00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.480
Now, that was a fakie shower.

125
00:04:59.480 --> 00:04:59.980
Oh.

126
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.880
That was a design by our set designer, Michael Gallenberg.

127
00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.580
Because there was a real shower down there.

128
00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:07.700
I had to use it one time.

129
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.500
Not in that room.

130
00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.080
Across the hallway, there was an office that had a tiny shower.

131
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.020
Really?

132
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:14.800
Yes.

133
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.680
Why did you have to use it?

134
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:17.440
Okay.

135
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.500
Well, do you remember the summer my hair turned green?

136
00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:20.960
Yep.

137
00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:22.700
You could really see it on camera.

138
00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.180
Guys, I spent the whole summer swimming with the kids in the pool and my hair turned green.

139
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.800
It happens to the blondies out there.

140
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.380
And Kim put this thing on my hair to, like, kind of tint it, you know?

141
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.420
To get the green out?

142
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:36.520
To get the green out.

143
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.960
And I had to wash it out of my hair.

144
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.180
Like, you put it on and then I had to rinse it out.

145
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.380
And then she had to blow dry my hair.

146
00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:42.980
Okay.

147
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.140
Now, why didn't they just use the little sink in the hair and makeup trailer or the shower in your trailer?

148
00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.220
Because the sink in the hair and makeup trailer, first of all, it didn't get hot.

149
00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:03.200
And Kim was a little concerned because the water is like this sort of recycled water that it would strip the color off my hair that she was trying to put in.

150
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:09.480
Now that you say that, I know that because there have been times when I've needed to shower off.

151
00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.560
Your trailers have a shower.

152
00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.260
But it is exactly that.

153
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.940
It's like a tank of weird sort of chemically treated water.

154
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:17.320
Right.

155
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.500
So that it can stay in the tank and then not be poisonous.

156
00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.040
Yeah.

157
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:23.260
And it doesn't get very hot.

158
00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.040
And also, I've had people tell me not to use it.

159
00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.860
I sort of was like, maybe just get rid of the showers and the trailers.

160
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:31.580
Yeah, put in a closet.

161
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:32.320
They're taking up space.

162
00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:33.720
We all need a closet.

163
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.240
Always need more closet.

164
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.960
I mean, it's part of my life mantra.

165
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:38.200
Can I have more closets?

166
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.340
Well, you need closets for your baskets, lady.

167
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:42.880
I do.

168
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.760
Your basket collection.

169
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:44.920
Give me all the baskets.

170
00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.060
Well, listen, this little room, it was located in our writer's building.

171
00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.520
It was on the bottom floor.

172
00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.260
And it was an actual storage closet.

173
00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.000
It was not a set.

174
00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.660
So the walls did not move.

175
00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.220
They talked about, should we build Michael Scott Paper Company in the warehouse?

176
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.460
That was my thought.

177
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.460
Where we've built many a set.

178
00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:08.520
No.

179
00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:13.720
For whatever reason, they crammed us into the post-production storage room,

180
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:20.700
where previous to us shooting in there, they had stored thousands of hours of archived

181
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:21.980
tape of our show.

182
00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.100
This was back in the day.

183
00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:29.940
And we just had walls and walls of five seasons of tape on our show.

184
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.340
They had to relocate all the tapes.

185
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.140
They had to redress the room.

186
00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.020
I said, the shower is fake, but those pipes were real.

187
00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.340
There really were these giant pipes.

188
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:40.680
Yes.

189
00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.400
The big black pipes that were coming out, those were real.

190
00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:44.000
Yes.

191
00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:50.220
Also, the bathrooms that are outside in the hallway, the two doors on either side of the

192
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.120
Michael Scott Paper Company door, those bathrooms were real.

193
00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.620
Those were the actual working bathrooms for anybody who worked on the bottom floor.

194
00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.240
Those were the bathrooms we would use in between scenes when we filmed in the warehouse.

195
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:01.680
Yes.

196
00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.640
Or in that hallway by the editing bay.

197
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:09.020
Right across from us were our two editors, Claire and Dave.

198
00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.220
And above us were the writers' offices.

199
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.300
So we were over in that building with the writers.

200
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.380
Also in this building is the lobby area that leads out into the parking lot.

201
00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.680
This room also gets repurposed for Cafe Disco.

202
00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:25.220
Yes.

203
00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:26.960
We went back down there for that.

204
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.400
So those are all my details on that room.

205
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.940
I'll have more to share as we break down the episode.

206
00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.080
More little quirks of working in there.

207
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:34.500
Yeah.

208
00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.980
You spent a lot of time in there.

209
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:36.460
We did.

210
00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.100
Are you ready for fast fact number two?

211
00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:39.960
I am.

212
00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:47.460
Remember last week when I told you that Michael sent a letter to everybody through NBC.com

213
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:47.680
Yes.

214
00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.560
Announcing his new paper company?

215
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:58.700
Well, this week he unveiled his website and NBC created a Michael Scott paper company website

216
00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.940
and it had photos of the room.

217
00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.500
It was basically the same room taken from three different angles.

218
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:11.860
It had the downloadable coupon that is shown in this episode.

219
00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.320
You know, the one that promises unparalleled customer service.

220
00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:15.660
Yep.

221
00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.200
That Pam and Ryan refused to copy.

222
00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.320
And it also had the mission statement for Michael Scott paper company.

223
00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:24.820
Yes, it did.

224
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.460
And we reached out to Joya.

225
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:27.980
You know, we've talked about Joya before.

226
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:33.940
She was in charge of all the digital content for the office that was on NBC.com.

227
00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.080
She shared with us what the mission statement said.

228
00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:38.800
Here it is.

229
00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.940
It's the Michael Scott paper company's mission to provide you the highest quality paper,

230
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.360
the best possible service at the lowest possible prices.

231
00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.420
Sounds good.

232
00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:49.440
Just wait.

233
00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:50.460
There's more.

234
00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.040
This paper business is more than just paper.

235
00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:54.560
More than just a business.

236
00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:59.040
It's about establishing quality human relationships with our clients.

237
00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:04.560
While little Timmy wakes up in the middle of the night with whooping cough and you need someone

238
00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.260
to call, we'll be there.

239
00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.760
When sweet Christine gets stood up by her prom date, we'll be there.

240
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.480
When the dam breaks in your backyard and the only thing that can stop the flow of water

241
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.180
from flooding your house is a ream of paper, we'll be there.

242
00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.600
At the Michael Scott paper company, we'll always be there.

243
00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:30.220
Through thick and thin, in sickness and health, till death or retirement do us part, we'll be there.

244
00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.780
For a while, it's just pretty standard.

245
00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:35.780
Kind of boilerplate.

246
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:36.040
Yeah.

247
00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:36.460
Yeah.

248
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.860
But I really like it when he says we're more than just a business.

249
00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.400
We're about quality human relationships with our clients.

250
00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.320
I'm like, oh.

251
00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:50.040
And then it goes off into some very specific examples of when you might be there for me.

252
00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:50.540
Mm-hmm.

253
00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.280
But in the end, I liked it.

254
00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:56.800
I think I was a little curious about what kind of backyard you have that has a dam.

255
00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:01.020
Well, listen, we'll be there.

256
00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.660
Fast fact number three, Ellie Kemper is our new receptioniSt.

257
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:07.140
Woo-hoo!

258
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.100
We love Ellie.

259
00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.320
We are definitely going to have her on the show.

260
00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.860
You know, Ellie and I both grew up in St. Louis.

