00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.440
James Carey had the candy bag for this episode.

336
00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:41.320
And it included Aaron's two talking heads.

337
00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:43.400
Neither of which end up in the episode.

338
00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.300
Well, one of them is on the DVD.

339
00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:52.740
Okay, this is the one that was in the candy bag as their first choice, which was, it's pretty cool.

340
00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:54.960
Like any other office, I guess.

341
00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:58.380
Phones, computers, copiers.

342
00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.020
Do I have to do this on all my breaks?

343
00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:01.600
Yeah!

344
00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.040
Is that the one that's included?

345
00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.500
Yeah, and I was a little confused because I thought it felt sort of off.

346
00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.400
Then they had this must shoot alternate, which went like this.

347
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:14.480
Most places you go, the chairs are really crappy and you're uncomfortable all day.

348
00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.340
My chair here is great.

349
00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:18.660
I really hope this works out.

350
00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.520
And that was like the one that quirky Aaron would say.

351
00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:23.220
Yeah.

352
00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.400
I guess that's our sign that the office got the new chairs.

353
00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:28.500
Because she's loving Pam's chair.

354
00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:29.320
That's it.

355
00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:33.300
I think Pam finally got her new chair and now she's working down in Michael Scott Paper Company.

356
00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:35.600
Pam talks about the chairs too later on.

357
00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:36.100
I know.

358
00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:42.680
I want to say that this doing two versions of your character happened to me on the film Blades of Glory.

359
00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:43.500
Really?

360
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:48.620
Yeah, I had gone in with this idea for this version of the character that was based kind

361
00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.500
of on like Bernadette Peters' performance in The Jerk.

362
00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:52.080
Yeah.

363
00:16:52.260 --> 00:17:00.900
I just really loved her wide-eyed, innocent, earnest approach to love and sex and just sort

364
00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.600
of all of it and being with sort of an oddball guy.

365
00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:08.420
And that is very similar to my character of Katie in Blades of Glory.

366
00:17:09.080 --> 00:17:13.560
But I think there was a little bit of concern that that might feel like too big of a choice.

367
00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.460
And so they also had me do all the scenes just pretty straight.

368
00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:22.140
And I did that for a couple of days, every scene.

369
00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:24.680
And eventually they went with my quirky version.

370
00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:25.920
I was so proud.

371
00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:33.320
But it was super intense because I remember one day I was shooting the scene with Will Arnett

372
00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:34.100
and Amy Poehler.

373
00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:37.160
They played a brother-sister ice skating duo.

374
00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:38.500
Yeah, I remember.

375
00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:40.420
And their little sister slash assistant.

376
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:41.660
Oh my God.

377
00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.760
It was so much fun shooting with those guys.

378
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.580
That whole movie looked like a blaSt.

379
00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:45.940
I know.

380
00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.300
But the cast looked like so much fun.

381
00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:48.860
We had a blaSt.

382
00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:55.520
But we had this scene and they were like, okay, we're going to lunch.

383
00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.980
Jenna, you get two takes.

384
00:17:57.740 --> 00:17:59.980
One in one version and one in the other.

385
00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:02.440
And I had to just nail both versions.

386
00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:07.400
And I remember someone, and I can't remember who, came up to me early and they were like,

387
00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.000
just don't do a good job of the version you don't want to have them pick.

388
00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:16.120
Like, just do the version you like really well.

389
00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:16.600
Tank one of them.

390
00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:16.920
Yeah.

391
00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:21.500
And then they'll be forced to go with, you know, your vision of the character.

392
00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:23.440
I couldn't do it.

393
00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:24.720
My, like, actor integrity.

394
00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:25.360
No.

395
00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.000
Just your whole moral compass.

396
00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:28.240
You couldn't ever do that.

397
00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:28.540
Yeah, I couldn't do it.

398
00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:29.220
I couldn't do it.

399
00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:34.420
It is interesting to me how characters become, like, who they are on a show or in a movie.

400
00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:38.200
This is reminding me of a project that one of my friends wrote.

401
00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.300
It was based on a real-life story from her life.

402
00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.860
And she had this character of a guy named Ed.

403
00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:48.060
And he was based on her friend in real life, Ed.

404
00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:50.220
And Ed was also an actor.

405
00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.440
So Ed went in to audition for Ed.

406
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:54.360
So he's in front of the network.

407
00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.900
And he's like, hi, I'm Ed.

408
00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.180
I'm reading for Ed.

409
00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.340
And he just was himself.

410
00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.000
She wrote him as himself.

411
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:04.180
We were all like, Ed's going to get the part of Ed, right?

412
00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:05.420
I mean, they wrote it for Ed.

413
00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:07.800
And he didn't get it.

414
00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:10.580
I was like, how can Ed not get Ed?

415
00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:11.780
It's based on Ed.

416
00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:13.120
Like, what?

417
00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:15.420
Lady, I have a very similar story.

418
00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:19.460
Okay, I auditioned for this lead role in a pilot opposite Dan Finnerty.

419
00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.540
He's from the Dan Band.

420
00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:21.060
Yeah, yeah.

421
00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:22.360
It was hilarious.

422
00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:28.460
And when I read the role, I thought to myself, oh, this is so Mary Lynn Reiskub.

423
00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:34.460
And so I went in and I just did my best impression of Mary Lynn.

424
00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:35.800
I love Mary Lynn.

425
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:36.420
I know.

426
00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:37.720
I have worked with her many times.

427
00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:38.240
I love her.

428
00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:39.140
I super copied her.

429
00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.080
I get to the final audition.

430
00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.740
It's me and two other women.

431
00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:46.520
One of them is like a tall, gorgeous blonde.

432
00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.580
The other one is Mary Lynn Reiskub.

433
00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:55.860
Then I find out that Dan knows Mary Lynn and he wrote the role for her.

434
00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.140
Any wonder?

435
00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.900
Well, I was proud of myself because I was like, well, I guess I nailed it on what he was going for.

436
00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.520
He was going for Mary Lynn.

437
00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:05.920
Did Mary Lynn get it?

438
00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:07.380
No, neither of us did.

439
00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.020
They gave it to the blonde.

440
00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:10.240
Oh, no.

441
00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:11.160
I know.

442
00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:15.780
And it was so funny because I thought to myself, well, obviously, they're going to go with Mary Lynn.

443
00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.260
They're not going to go with me.

444
00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:18.420
I'm copying her.

445
00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:19.000
Right.

446
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:20.680
They didn't give it to either of us.

447
00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.580
But the two of us were at least doing what he intended when he wrote it.

448
00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:25.120
Yeah.

449
00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:26.700
No, they went the other way.

450
00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:28.400
You never know.

451
00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:29.220
You never know.

452
00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:31.060
Well, why don't we take a break?

